
Glossary:
OpenMS - Name of the project and our C++ library
TOPP - "The OpenMS PiPeline", a collection of chainable tools for flexible HPLC/MS workflows. Formerly known as "The OpenMS Proteomics Pipeline"
TOPPAS - "The OpenMS PiPeline Assistant", a graphical tool to interactively build and run HPLC/MS workflows
TOPPView - Versatile viewer for HPLC/MS data
INI file - parameter configuration file, holding custom parameter settings for TOPP tools
INIFileEditor - graphical parameter editor for INI files
Parameters - list of algorithm or TOPP tool parameters that changed in this release
PR - Pull Request (on GitHub), i.e. integration of a new feature or bugfix
#<number>, e.g. #4957 - a reference to an issue or pull request on GitHub, visit e.g. https://github.com/OpenMS/OpenMS/pull/XXXX (replace XXXX with number of interest) for details



------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
----                                OpenMS 3.6.0     (under development)              ----
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

General:
  - Fix: corrected inconsistent elemental formulas in several NuXL presets, including RNA/DNA UV,
    DEB, NM, and FA fragment adducts (#10031)
  - Qt is no longer needed outside the GUI: the core library and all non-GUI TOPP tools build without
    it, and Qt6::Core was removed from libOpenMS' PUBLIC CMake dependencies (see Dependencies)
  - BREAKING: the OpenMS::String and OpenMS::StringView classes were removed; OpenMS uses
    std::string / std::string_view throughout its public API and internals (StringList is now
    std::vector<std::string>). Member helpers moved to free functions in OpenMS::StringUtils (toStr,
    split, trim/trimmed, substitute/substituted, toInt/toDouble/toFloat, number, ...); the
    String/StringConversions/StringUtilsSimple shim headers were removed (#9450, #9468)
  - BREAKING: LibSVM, Xerces-C++ and SQLite are now PRIVATE dependencies of libOpenMS -- no installed
    header exposes their types, so downstream projects no longer need their include paths. XMLHandler
    no longer derives from xercesc::DefaultHandler; subclasses must migrate to the native callbacks
    onStartElement(const char16_t*, const XMLAttributes&) / onEndElement() / onCharacters()
    (#8547, #8558, #9712, #9737)
  - Arrow/Parquet is now a required dependency on all platforms; the WITH_PARQUET CMake option was
    removed (#8370, #8422, #8991)
  - macOS Intel builds discontinued and the deployment target raised to macOS 14; Ubuntu CI runners
    updated to 24.04; macOS code signing and notarization are done in CI (#8486, #8494, #8527)
  - New raw data readers: Bruker TimsTOF .d (BRUKER_TDF), Thermo .raw, imzML and Bruker MALDI imaging
    (see OpenMS Library and TOPP tools)
  - New native Parquet formats .idparquet, .featureparquet and .consensusparquet for lossless
    identification, feature and consensus map storage, supported by FileHandler and ~20 TOPP tools
    (#9225, #9232, #9236, #9237, #9241, #9242, #9396, #9405)
  - Transparent compression: .zip input for XML-based formats and Bruker .d directories is decompressed
    on the fly, and every XML-based format can be written as .gz or .bz2 by using the corresponding
    extension (e.g. "output.idXML.gz") (#7560, #9139, #9259)
  - Fix: shared data (share/OpenMS) is now resolved deterministically from the compiled-in path, then
    relative to the executable, and only as a last resort from OPENMS_DATA_PATH; a stale environment
    variable or an old side-by-side install can no longer hijack resolution (most relevant on Windows).
    File::find reports the resolved data directory and where it came from when a file is missing
    (#9635, #9636, #9650)
  - The class-test framework (ClassTest.h macros, FuzzyStringComparator) was moved out of libOpenMS
    into its own standard-library-only static library, OpenMSTestFramework. Include paths and
    installed header locations are unchanged; the ~50 framework symbols leave the libOpenMS ABI, and
    the FuzzyDiff tool and all class tests link the new library. Tests of libraries that do not link
    libOpenMS (e.g. OpenSwathAlgo) now build without libOpenMS in their link closure. Schema
    validation of NEW_TMP_FILE outputs is now explicit (VALIDATE_TMP_FILES; the 26 tests that
    validated files call it), unique-ID seeding and OpenMS exception naming are registered by the
    test projects (OpenMSTestSupport.cpp), and the unused Internal::OpenMS_locale global was removed
    (#9929)
  - Fix: File::writable() is no longer destructive and no longer reports false negatives under
    concurrency. It used to answer "does it exist?" and "may I write it?" with two separate
    queries, so a concurrent process could change the path in between and make it wrongly report
    a writable path as unwritable; and it probed a non-existent path by creating that very file
    and deleting it again, which could unlink output another process had just written there.
    It now asks once and probes the target *directory* with a uniquely named temporary file.
    File::readable() lost the same redundant exists() check. This removes a source of
    intermittent "Cannot write output file" failures when two TOPP tools share an output path
    (#9937, #9940, #9941)
  - Fix: MRMFeatureSelector selected no features at all on builds using the GLPK backend
    (-DLP_SOLVER=GLPK, or the vcpkg "lp-glpk" feature). Its "exactly one feature per transition"
    constraints were typed DOUBLE_BOUNDED with both bounds equal, which reaches GLPK as GLP_DB
    with lb == ub; glp_intopt then returns GLP_EBOUND and leaves every column at zero, and the
    unchecked return value turned that into a silently empty selection. They are now typed FIXED.
    No effect on the COIN-OR and HiGHS backends, which treat the two types identically (#9940)
  - Fix: LP/ILP solver failures are no longer silent. LPWrapper::getStatus() now reports the real
    solution status on the COIN-OR backend (it unconditionally returned UNDEFINED there, and the raw
    solve() return value cannot signal failure: COIN-OR and HiGHS report a completed run even for a
    proven-infeasible model). LPWrapper::solve() logs a warning whenever no feasible solution was
    produced, and its callers (MRMFeatureSelector, ILPDCWrapper/Decharger) now check getStatus() and
    throw an informative exception instead of reading the all-zero non-solution back as valid output
    - previously any solver failure (infeasible model, bad bounds, resource limit) yielded silently
    empty results (#9944, #9956)
  - BREAKING (pyOpenMS): the low-level LPWrapper class (with its SolverParam and nested enums) is no
    longer exposed in pyOpenMS. It is an internal LP/MILP backend wrapper with no Python consumers and
    an easy-to-misuse solve()/getStatus() contract; use the high-level algorithms (MRMFeatureSelector,
    FeatureDecharger, ...) from Python instead (#9983)
  - Removed two unused example scripts from share/OpenMS/SCRIPTS: ropenms.R (superseded by the
    "pyOpenMS in R" user guide) and ProteomicsLFQ.R (a stale iPRG2015-specific MSstats example).
    The InternalCalibration_*.R scripts invoked by the calibration tools and plot_trafo.R remain
    (#9945)
  - Removed the unregistered developer runners under src/tests/topp/ProteomicsLFQTestScripts,
    which no CTest target ran (run_lfq.sh and run_BSA.sh were additionally hardwired to personal
    machine paths), and the ProteomicsLFQ documentation line that pointed to them (#9945)
  - Cleanup: pruned share/OpenMS/SCHEMAS from 62 to 23 XSD files, keeping only the schemas OpenMS
    actually loads at runtime (the current version each format's *File class validates against when
    isValid() is called, their <xs:include> closure, the four auto-detected mzIdentML versions, and
    Param_1_7_0 used for CTD/CWL export). The 39 removed files (superseded format versions plus the
    never-loaded CvMapping, CTD_0_3, qcML_0.0.7, mzQCML_0_0_5 and mzQuantML_1_0_0-rc2) remain
    available from the release/3.5.0 tag; a new SCHEMAS/README.md documents what is kept and where
    the archived schemas live. Also fixed two dangling schema references that pointed at
    non-existent files: QcMLFile (qcml.xsd) and PepXMLFileMascot (PepXML_1_8.xsd) now declare no
    bundled schema -- since both paths were already broken, their isValid() (which nothing in the
    codebase calls) now throws NotImplemented rather than a file-not-found error, i.e. semantic
    schema validation stays unavailable for these two formats. Normal file reading/writing is
    unaffected (#9757)

Dependencies:
  - PyOpenMS: Autowrap/Cython replaced with nanobind; nanobind 2.10.0+ fetched automatically (#8699)
  - Eigen minimum version updated from 3.3.4 to 3.4.0 (#8366)
  - Boost minimum version requirement raised from 1.78 to 1.81 (#9042, #9095)
  - Qt6::Network replaced with libcurl for all HTTP operations in the core library (#8841)
  - minizip-ng replaced with libzip for ZIP64 archive support (#8808)
  - Qt6 removed from the core library and from all non-GUI TOPP tools (incremental steps, Qt is now
    only required with WITH_GUI=ON):
    - QDateTime/QDate/QSysInfo/QLocale replaced with std::chrono and platform APIs in Date, DateTime,
      BuildInfo, DimMapper and TOPPBase; the QDate constructor was removed from Date (#8937)
    - QDir/QFile/QFileInfo replaced with std::filesystem in File, PathUtils, ExperimentalDesignFile and
      others; copyDirRecursively/removeDir no longer take QString&, ToolHandler returns StringList; new
      PathUtils.h with a UTF-8-safe to_path() helper (#8938)
    - QProcess/QObject replaced with boost::process in ExternalProcess, PythonInfo, JavaInfo and
      RWrapper; ExternalProcess no longer inherits from QObject and the public APIs use
      String/vector<String> (#8939)
    - QJsonDocument/QJsonObject replaced with nlohmann/json in OMSFileLoad (#8936)
    - Qt string interop removed from String, DataValue, StringUtils and StringListUtils;
      Qt5Port.h deleted (#8940)
    - Qt6::Core removed from libOpenMS PUBLIC CMake link dependencies; downstream CMake projects must
      link Qt6::Core directly if they use Qt (#8965)
    - ExecutePipeline, INIUpdater and ImageCreator moved from src/topp/ to the GUI library (#9041)
  - boost-process added as a header-only dependency for cross-platform subprocess management (#8939)
  - ENABLE_TDL CMake option (required for CWL/Common Workflow Language support) now defaults to OFF;
    set -DENABLE_TDL=ON to re-enable (#9067)
  - contrib is now a git submodule; building from a git clone requires `git submodule update --init`
    (or cloning with `--recurse-submodules`) (#9155)
  - opentims: CMake now searches for a system-installed opentims_cpp via FindOpentims.cmake before
    falling back to a FetchContent clone; set opentims_DIR/opentims_ROOT to skip the download
    (#9147, #9148)
  - wnetalign/wnet/pylmcf: three header-only libraries fetched via FetchContent for Wasserstein network
    alignment; controlled by the WITH_WNETALIGN CMake option (ON by default) (#8992)
  - HiGHS added as a third LP backend next to COIN-OR and GLPK, selectable via the LP_SOLVER CMake
    variable (AUTO/COIN/GLPK/HIGHS); AUTO tries COIN-OR, then GLPK, then fetches HiGHS (#9246)
  - Fix: CMake accepts Arrow/Parquet 24.x and later (both the main and the pyOpenMS standalone build);
    the find_package version pin was dropped in favour of a VERSION_LESS guard on the >= 23 minimum
    (#8679, #9196, #9221)

PyOpenMS:
  - BREAKING: binding backend replaced from Autowrap/Cython by hand-maintained nanobind bindings (#8699)
    - Python-level API is backward compatible (same class names, signatures, return types)
    - 544 classes wrapped across 10 domain-based modules for parallel compilation
    - custom type casters for OpenMS::String, DataValue, DPosition, etc.; addon system preserved
      (pure Python methods injected via the @addon("ClassName") decorator)
    - PEP 561 type stubs (.pyi), smaller binaries, faster builds and better error messages
    - along the way: ~50 mutable-reference output parameter bugs fixed and ~100 missing default
      arguments restored
  - BREAKING: getters return owned copies again, restoring the 3.5 (Cython) semantics that the initial
    nanobind port had turned into live aliases (#9792, #9794)
    - element access (spec[i], for peak in spec, exp.getSpectrum(i)), vector getters (getSpectra(),
      getHits(), getPrecursors(), getFloatDataArrays(), ...) and single sub-object getters
      (getInstrumentSettings(), getPrecursor(), getParameters(), ...) all hand out copies
    - one rule for every getter: read it, edit it, put it back -- e.g.
      s = exp.getSpectrum(0); s.setRT(1.0); exp[0] = s
    - closes the use-after-free and silent-corruption classes from #9792: an alias no longer dangles
      when the container reallocates (push_back) and no longer silently re-labels a different element
      when the container is sorted (sortByRT); AASequence element access no longer exposes mutable
      handles into the process-wide ResidueDB
    - see src/pyOpenMS/OWNERSHIP.md for the full rule and the three documented exceptions (fluent
      builders, database lookups, and shared DataProcessing entries)
    - opt-in aliasing access is available under an explicit naming scheme: <singular>_view(i) for one
      element (spectrum_view(i), feature_view(i), chromatogram_view(i)), <singular>_views() for the
      collection, and iter_<singular>_views() for iteration; the get_ prefix is reserved for owned
      copies, so aliasing accessors never carry it; edits through a view land
      immediately, the parent is kept alive, and resizing/reordering the list invalidates the view.
      Available on MSExperiment, FeatureMap, ConsensusMap, PeptideIdentificationList,
      MRMTransitionGroup and the MSSpectrum/MSChromatogram data arrays; the built-in DataFrame
      exports iterate views internally (spectrum iteration ~100x faster than copying)
  - FIX: MSSpectrum.set_drift_time_array() no longer frees the buffer a live numpy view points into
    when the replacement array has the same length (#9792)
  - SpectrumAccessOpenMSCached wrapped (on-disk cached spectrum access for OpenSWATH-style workflows);
    SimpleOpenMSSpectraFactory.getSpectrumAccessOpenMSPtr() now returns it for experiments carrying
    the cached_data marker instead of raising (#9952)
  - BREAKING: metadata enums converted to strongly typed enum classes (#8604); activation methods enum
    handling changed (#8405)
  - BREAKING: DataFrame methods renamed to Pythonic conventions (get_df() -> to_df(), get_df_columns()
    -> df_columns(), ...) and zero-copy _mv methods renamed to _view (get_data_mv() -> data_view());
    deprecated aliases are provided for both (#8655, #8857)
  - DataFrame / Arrow support: to_df() for MSSpectrum, MSChromatogram, Mobilogram, MSExperiment,
    ConsensusMap, FeatureMap and PeptideIdentificationList, get_chromatogram_df()/get_feature_df() for
    MRMTransitionGroupCP, df_columns() for column discovery, Parquet export and zero-copy interop via
    pyarrow (#8436, #8520, #8632, #8655, #8729)
  - Pythonic API: __len__(), __str__()/__repr__(), __hash__() for 40+ classes, append()/extend() for
    ConsensusMap/FeatureMap/PeptideIdentificationList, dict-like Param.to_dict(), and snake_case
    properties over the scalar getters/setters of the kernel classes (MSSpectrum.rt, .ms_level,
    .drift_time, .native_id, ...; Peak1D.mz, .intensity) -- purely additive, the get*/set* methods are
    unchanged (#8501, #8503, #8510, #8576, #8631, #9760)
  - Strings: functions accept both str and bytes via libcpp_utf8_string (#8602, #8604)
  - New wrappers: ProFormaParser (full ProForma v2: parse/serialize with LOSSLESS/CANONICAL modes,
    AASequence conversion policies, mass calculation, JSON round-trip) (#8648); USI (#8684);
    PEFFFile/PEFFEntry/PEFFDatabaseMetadata for PSI Extended FASTA (#8690); XICParquetFile and
    XIMParquetFile (#8737, #8738, #8894); BrukerTimsFile incl. its nested Config (gated by
    WITH_OPENTIMS, feature-detectable via hasattr) (#9379); FileInfo for programmatic file inspection
    (#9602); IsoelectricPoint (#9419); FAIMSHelper (#9275); SpectrumNativeIDParser (#8637);
    complete FileHandler wrapper with 14 load/store methods (#8762); MSExperiment.rasterize2D() (#8739);
    MZTrafoModel.setRANSACSeed() (#9407); PeptideAndProteinQuant result accessors (#8543);
    IDConflictResolverAlgorithm::resolveAllHitRankAggregation() (#8907); EmpiricalFormula,
    Residue and TheoreticalSpectrumGenerator additions (#9412, #9415); Math.getPPM()/getPPMAbs()
    (#8525); DRange1 (#8504)
  - Ion mobility: the per-peak IM dimension can now be written, not just read -- set_im_data() /
    set_drift_time_array() take the unit explicitly instead of requiring the magic data-array name;
    get_drift_time_array()/get_drift_time_unit() accessors and zero-copy peaks_struct() for
    MSChromatogram and Mobilogram (#8423, #8825, #8842, #9792)
  - Static methods modernized with the @staticmethod decorator (#8562); MSSpectrum.getType(bool)
    wrapped (#8589)
  - Fix: use-after-free in the bindings. __iter__ handed out iterators that did not keep their container
    alive, the mzML/mzXML/imzML consumer callbacks handed Python a reference into the parser's batch
    buffer that was recycled on return, and 21 registrations stored borrowed pointers that could outlive
    their referent (four unusable classes were removed). Iterators and consumers now keep their owner
    alive, retained pointers were converted to owning copies, and out-of-range accessors raise IndexError
    (#9792, #9794)
  - Fix: set_peaks() replaced the peak table without touching the parallel binary data arrays, leaving
    the container silently inconsistent (annotations no longer describing their peaks, mis-sized mzML
    binary arrays); the data arrays are now resized/cleared along with the peaks (#9792, #9795)
  - Fix: get_drift_time_unit() returned the scalar per-spectrum unit even when guarding on the per-peak
    array, so it reported NONE for correctly labelled frames; both representations are now handled (#9792)
  - Fix: the zero-copy peaks_struct() dtype derived its field offsets instead of reading them, so an
    ABI change would have gone unnoticed; offsets are now taken from the C++ members (#9792)
  - Fix: AASequence.__str__() returns the peptide sequence instead of the repr format, and copy
    constructor dispatch was corrected for AASequence and 11 further classes (ProteinHit, Software,
    CVTerm, PeptideEvidence, USI, Ribonucleotide, ...) so copies no longer round-trip through strings
    (#9098)
  - Fix: audit of all 108 nb::enum_ registrations against their C++ definitions found 4 enums with
    missing enumerators (NASequence::NASFragmentType, ProteomicsPkaScale, OSWHierarchy::Level,
    LPWrapper::SOLVER); existing bound values were unaffected (#9705)
  - New src/pyOpenMS/THREAD_SAFETY.md documents the binding layer's thread-safety contract: releasing
    the GIL around a call is not a thread-safety guarantee, the concurrency rules callers must follow,
    an inventory of the 62 nb::gil_scoped_release sites, and how to avoid OpenMP oversubscription when
    combining Python-level threading/multiprocessing with OpenMS' internal parallelism (#9792, #9857)
  - Fix: FileTypes wrapping updated with missing enum values and static methods (#8720); backward
    compatibility restored for the file I/O methods of IdXMLFile, PepXMLFile and MzIdentMLFile (#8553);
    getAAFrequencies() loop bug in the AASequence addon (#8502)
  - Fix: a build with -DWITH_WNETALIGN=OFF produced a pyOpenMS that could not be imported at all
    (undefined WNetMatcher symbol in _pyopenms_misc), because the WNet bindings referenced symbols
    that are excluded from libOpenMS by that option. The WNetMatcher and FeatureGroupingAlgorithmWNet
    bindings are now compiled only when WNet support is built, mirroring the BrukerTimsFile /
    WITH_OPENTIMS pattern, and feature-detectable via hasattr(pyopenms, "WNetMatcher") (#9946, #9957)
  - Docs: type hints in the docstrings of 133 functions (#8483), docstring format validation tests
    (#8674), 6 docutils ERROR-level warnings resolved (#9629), and corrected/added MSChromatogram and
    Mobilogram docstrings (#9812)
  - Build system modernized with py-build-cmake and cibuildwheel for PEP 517/518/621 compliance (#8530)

TOPP tools:
  Changes:
    all:
      - BREAKING: when a parameter is given several times on the command line, the LAST occurrence now
        wins (previously the first); a warning is issued when duplicates are detected. This enables the
        nextflow pattern of appending ${ext.args} to override hardcoded defaults. Error messages for
        command-line parameter problems were improved (#8670, fixes #5345)
      - Parquet I/O: .idparquet is read/written by IDFileConverter, IDMerger, IDRipper, IDFilter,
        IDScoreSwitcher, PeptideIndexer, PSMFeatureExtractor, PercolatorAdapter, CometAdapter,
        MSGFPlusAdapter, SageAdapter, MapRTTransformer, MapAlignerIdentification and
        FeatureFinderIdentification; .featureparquet/.consensusparquet by FileConverter,
        IDConflictResolver, MapRTTransformer, MapAlignerIdentification, FeatureFinderIdentification and
        the five FeatureLinker* tools; TextExporter and MzTabExporter read all three (load-only).
        Output format follows input format (#9232, #9236, #9237, #9241, #9396, #9405)
      - Thermo .raw input (requires WITH_THERMO_RAW=ON) for FileConverter, ProSE, SimpleSearchEngine,
        CometAdapter, SageAdapter, ProteomicsLFQ and OpenSwathWorkflow; files are loaded in-process via
        ThermoRawFile, with a temporary mzML only where an external search engine needs one (#9418)
      - Bruker TimsTOF .d input without a prior conversion step for FileConverter, CometAdapter,
        ProteomicsLFQ, OpenSwathWorkflow and PeakPickerIM (requires WITH_TIMSRUST=ON)
        (#8975, #9019, #9022, #9030, #9126)
      - IM_PEAK (per-peak ion mobility) input: PeakPickerHiRes warns and falls back to an
        intensity-weighted mean IM per picked peak; Resampler, FeatureFinderCentroided,
        FeatureFinderMultiplex and MultiplexResolver reject it with INCOMPATIBLE_INPUT_DATA (#9018)
    ProteomicsLFQ, IsobaricWorkflow, ProteinQuantifier, ProSE (QPX export):
      - BREAKING: -out_feature_qpx (single file) replaced by -out_qpx (directory), which writes a
        QPX/quantms collection of quantms.psm.parquet, quantms.feature.parquet and quantms.pg.parquet;
        ProSE additionally writes per-input <basename>.psm/.pg.parquet (#8973, #8974, #9146)
      - all three views now follow the QPX 1.1 contract and are joinable: long format (one row per
        entity and run), run_file_name as the bare spectrum file stem, and a pg view keyed on
        (pg_accessions, grouped_runs, label) with scalar label/intensity columns replacing the former
        nested intensities column. grouped_runs maps exactly to ExperimentalDesign::fraction_group, so
        samples reused across technical-replicate fraction groups no longer join those groups
        (#9816, #9818, #9825, #9843)
      - all three views now carry the mandatory QPX identity column (feature_id / psm_id / pg_id, int64,
        non-null, the view's primary key), derived exactly as the qpx reference implementation derives
        it -- BLAKE2b-64 over the canonical JSON of a footer-declared identity_composite. Matching it
        byte for byte is what lets a collection survive `qpxc convert openms`, which re-derives
        feature_id but does not rewrite the psm.feature_id pointing at it. The pg identity keys on the
        group's FULL membership, (pg_accessions, grouped_runs, label), not on its leading protein:
        two distinct groups sharing a leader would otherwise derive one pg_id, and since that column
        is the view's primary key the collision refuses the export rather than merely mislabelling a
        row. Both list members are hashed as sets -- deduplicated and ordered by their JSON encoding
        -- so inference order and design order do not enter the identity (#9864)
      - the feature and psm views are now explicitly cross-referenced: psm.feature_id and
        feature.psm_ids. A consensus feature emits one row per run, so its identifications are
        distributed to the row of their own run; an unassigned identification keeps feature_id null.
        Both directions are produced in one pass, so they always describe the same edge (#9864)
      - exports are validated and transactional: required values, primary-key uniqueness, channel-label
        agreement across runs, resolvable identification runs and the experimental-design contract are
        checked before the first file is opened, and a refused or failed export leaves no partial file or
        collection behind (#9817, #9832, #9834, #9835, #9843)
      - BREAKING: ProSE no longer writes a pg file when it inferred no protein groups, rather than
        writing an empty table that a consumer cannot distinguish from "inference found nothing"
      - Performance: the PSM and feature writers stream row batches through a persistent Parquet writer
        and build rows with OpenMP (n_threads: 1=serial default, 0=auto, N=fixed). On a 3M-PSM export
        peak RSS dropped from ~2.55 GB to ~0.15 GB and wall time from 18.0 s to 7.9 s at 4 threads;
        output is byte-identical for any thread count (#9692, #9697, #9698, #9699)
      - Removed the QPXConverter TOPP tool (registered but without callers or tests); the QPXFile export
        API it wrapped remains available (#9693)
      - Fix: .featureparquet now stores the FeatureMap's own unique id. Only the per-feature unique ids
        were written, so a map came back with an unset id -- ProteomicsLFQ, for one, copies that value
        into the consensus column header of the run the map came from. Files written before this keep
        the previous behaviour rather than failing to load
      - .featureparquet import now refuses a file in which a PSM references a feature unique id that is
        not in features.parquet, instead of warning and re-filing the identification as unassigned. The
        two identification lists partition the PSMs and which list one is in is part of the result: FDR
        is estimated over both together and every exporter reports them separately, so repairing the
        link silently produced a map that parsed cleanly and described a different experiment
    ProteinQuantifier (and the PeptideAndProteinQuant library class):
      - BREAKING: quantities are reported per assay -- the experimental-design (fraction_group, label)
        unit -- and the sample grain is gone. Previously all files mapped to a sample were summed, so
        declaring two runs of one sample as replicates changed a reported fold change from 6.4x to 11.9x
        on a BSA design. CSV columns are now abundance_fgroup<F>_label<L>; the consensusXML/idXML
        'indistinguishable_proteins_<N>_abundances' UserParam and the total_abundances /
        peptide_abundances members (incl. their pyOpenMS bindings) are removed in favour of
        fraction_group_abundances / peptide_fraction_group_abundances. MSstats and QPX pg
        values are unchanged, as they already read per-run or per-assay data (#9864, #9869)
      - BREAKING: how the fractions of a fraction group are combined is now a choice,
        'fractions:aggregate'. The default 'sum' is what every earlier version did (and what MaxQuant and
        Proteome Discoverer do); the new 'best' keeps a single fraction per peptide and fraction group,
        matching the de-duplication MSstats and FlashLFQ perform (#9876)
      - BREAKING: 'best_charge_and_fraction' renamed to 'best_charge' -- it selects a charge, never a
        fraction. There is no alias; old INI files and command lines fail as unknown parameter. It now
        selects one charge per modified peptide globally, by the number of assays with positive signal
        (ties broken by summed abundance, then lower charge), and keeps every observation of that charge
        instead of reducing the peptide to its single largest cell (#9796, #9864)
      - BREAKING: removed the opt-in '-ratios'/'-ratiosSILAC' flags and the 'ratio_1..N' /
        'SILACratio_1..3' CSV columns; use the abundance columns and form contrasts in a downstream
        statistics layer such as MSstats (#9853)
      - Fix: protein abundances aggregated peptides that were never detected, so a protein could be
        reported as absent from a channel it was measured in. IsobaricChannelExtractor writes a reporter
        it cannot find (or one below 'min_reporter_intensity') as an explicit 0.0 and inserts the handle
        anyway, and those entries both satisfied the "enough peptides" gate and dragged the default
        median aggregate towards zero. Fixed in all three aggregation levels (sample/assay, file+channel
        and fraction group) and in the 'consensus:fix_peptides' peptide ranking (#9796)
      - Fix: merged ID runs are now split by origin file. The per-PSM origin index was looked up under
        the misspelled key "id_merge_idx", so every PSM of a merged run was attributed to the first file
        and all other per-file samples stayed empty (#9658, fixes #5518)
      - Fix: the detailed peptide table ('-peptide_out' with 'best_charge') collapsed all fractions of a
        group onto the last file in the design (11 of 12 files disappeared from the header) and shifted
        row cells left whenever a value was missing
      - Perf: fixed quadratic hotspots in quantifyProteins() and replaced the per-call linear scan in
        getSampleIDFromFilenameAndChannel_() with a precomputed map; large isobaric runs (600 files x
        18 channels, ~7M PSMs) previously hung for hours at "Quantifying peptides". Output is
        bit-for-bit identical (#9672, #9683)
    ProSE:
      - BREAKING: renamed from PeptideDataBaseSearchFI; the old executable name is no longer available
        (#9137)
      - BREAKING: -out replaced by three dedicated outputs: -out_idxml (per-file idXML, must match the
        -in count), -out_qpx (QPX directory, see above) and -out_parquet (OpenMS-internal Parquet
        directory with PSM, protein, protein-group and search-params tables); unique input basenames are
        required for the two directory outputs (#9146)
      - BREAKING: precursor tolerance is now an asymmetric pair of non-negative magnitudes,
        'precursor:mass_tolerance_lower'/'_upper' (applied as [-lower, +upper], matching the
        MSFraggerAdapter convention); the scalar 'precursor:mass_tolerance' and
        'precursor:open_window_lower/_upper' are removed. Calibration now preserves signed precursor
        bias instead of collapsing asymmetric bounds to symmetric, and an extreme-bias guard discards
        implausible calibration results with a warning. Open search auto-detection trips above
        1000 ppm or 1 Da (#9108, #9132)
      - BREAKING: PSM UserParams matched_b_ions/matched_y_ions renamed to
        matched_prefix_ions/matched_suffix_ions (all a/b/c and x/y/z series are counted, not just b/y);
        delta_score and matched_ion_current added as PSM annotations and Percolator features; the
        HyperScore factorial boost now counts all prefix/suffix ions, improving ETD/EThcD scoring (#9175)
      - BREAKING: default precursor isotope error range changed from [-1, +1] to [0, +2] -- Orbitrap and
        timsTOF monoisotopic peak picking fails predominantly upward, so the symmetric range wasted a
        query slot; matches MetaMorpheus/MSFragger. isotope_error is now also a Percolator feature (#9188)
      - Changed default mass tolerances to typical high-resolution Orbitrap settings: precursor 20 ->
        10 ppm per side, fragment 10 -> 20 ppm. Data with calibration drift beyond 10 ppm may need a
        wider window or Search:calibration:enabled (#9634)
      - Multi-file search: -in accepts a file list, the fragment index is built once and reused across
        all inputs; new advanced -out_mod_analysis_dir writes per-file and aggregate
        modification-analysis TSVs (#9109)
      - Search modes: semi-tryptic and non-specific search via peptide:enzyme_specificity
        (full/semi/none), SNES mother-peptide indexing for non-specific searches (~2L instead of O(L^2)
        peptides per protein, opt-in via snes_enabled, mothers truncated at ambiguous residues X/B/Z),
        open search with modification discovery and FDR filtering, and FASTA chunking for databases whose
        index exceeds available memory (-Search:database:chunk_size)
        (#8414, #9112, #9177, #9191, #9224)
      - Automatic mass accuracy calibration (Search:calibration:enabled): a fast first-pass search on a
        TIC-ranked subset estimates tighter precursor and fragment tolerances without rebuilding the
        index. The pass is parallelized with OpenMP (wall-time overhead down from ~91% to ~7% on 20
        cores), samples strided across the whole database in chunked mode, and estimates tolerances from
        0.5%/99.5% empirical quantiles instead of median +- 3xMAD (#9122, #9177, #9185, #9639)
      - Auto decoy mode: Search:decoys is now a three-way enum (auto/generate/ignore, default auto).
        'auto' reuses existing decoys detected via DecoyHelper::findDecoyString (prefix and suffix
        markers) and generates them when none are found; this also fixes latent bugs where
        PeptideIndexing and picked-protein FDR assumed a hardcoded decoy-prefix position (#9634)
      - FDR: PSM-level FDR plus optional picked-protein FDR (Savitski 2015) via the new FDR:protein
        parameter. Both are deferred and applied to Percolator q-values when rescoring is configured
        (previously FDR:PSM stripped the decoys before Percolator ran, so Percolator was skipped
        entirely), and protein FDR now also runs for single-file searches. Protein FDR is never applied
        per file in multi-file mode, since picked-protein FDR is only valid on a complete protein set
        (#9123, #9195, #9633, #9644)
      - Percolator: -out_pin writes a valid, percolator-CLI-consumable .pin without requiring a
        -percolator_executable; ProSE and PercolatorAdapter share the feature set via the new
        PercolatorInfile::getStandardFeatureSet(). The sibling PercolatorAdapter binary is resolved via
        File::findSiblingTOPPExecutable instead of relying on $PATH, which previously caused a silent
        fallback to HyperScore results in dev builds (#9166, #9195, #9204)
      - Four additional Percolator features registered in extra_features (default set grows from 10 to
        14): hyperscore_zscore and ln_num_candidates (from the per-spectrum candidate pool already built
        during scoring), matched_ion_current_fraction (matched_ion_current normalised by TIC) and
        complementary_ions_fraction (fraction of cleavage sites with both prefix and complementary suffix
        ion matched). +0.4% to +1.2% target PSMs at 1% FDR after rescoring across three benchmark DDA
        runs (timsTOF, Q Exactive HF, Orbitrap Astral); rescoring itself remains the larger effect
        (+6.6% to +30.8%). Also fixes FragmentIndex's annotate:PSM valid-strings list, which was missing
        six annotation names already accepted by ProSEAlgorithm (#9970)
      - New annotate:PSM options (num_matched_peaks, matched_prefix/suffix_ions,
        longest_peptide_ion_sequence, fragment_annotation), runtime search diagnostics, and a
        consolidated end-of-search "Search Report" (configuration recap, index stats, per-file ID
        results, tolerance estimates, per-phase timing, output manifest) with an optional -summary_out
        (#9117, #9121, #9642)
      - FragmentIndex: default fragment:min_ion_index changed from 0 to 2 (skips b1/b2/y1/y2, matching
        Sage; improves PSM count and reduces memory and runtime), fixed bucket_size of 4096 instead of
        sqrt(N) (~11% faster on large immunopeptidomics indices), plus a bitmask-based modification
        enumeration and a SIMD-accelerated SoA query loop (#9088, #9094, #9203)
      - Fix: ions:add_a/b/c/x/y/z_ions are now forwarded to TheoreticalSpectrumGenerator in the scoring
        path; previously the index and the scorer disagreed (c/z ions were indexed but not scored),
        breaking ETD/EThcD searches (#9167)
      - Fix: the precursor_mz_error_ppm Percolator feature subtracts the matched isotope offset, and the
        fragment-error features are computed at the actual fragment tolerance instead of the 0.3 Da
        default (#9177)
      - Fix: a failed -out_merged write no longer discards the per-file outputs, and the merged idXML
        write no longer crashes with "Invalid protein reference 'DECOY_...'" when target+decoy shared
        peptides are present (#9197, #9205)
    PercolatorAdapter:
      - Fix: the tool aborted with "Executable not found (the executable 'percolator' could not be
        found)" on machines without percolator installed, even for the default in-process backend that
        never runs the binary. -percolator_executable carries the 'is_executable' tag, so reading it
        resolves it on PATH; it is now read only when the subprocess path will actually be used, which
        also restores the TOPP_PercolatorAdapter_1_inproc / _CalcMass tests in such builds (#10020)
      - Fix: an input CalcMass meta value (a search engine's way of stating that a PSM's precursor mass
        differs from its AASequence, which FeatureFinderIdentification's CalcMass correction consumes)
        was deleted along with the training-only PIN columns after in-process rescoring, even though
        PercolatorInfile only computes CalcMass for hits that arrive without one. -use_subprocess true
        already preserved it. The stamping step now records per hit which keys it actually added and
        strips only those, so both backends agree; other search engines' output is unaffected. On a
        real cross-linking dataset this restored label-free quantification of every cross-linked
        feature reaching 1% FDR through the default (in-process) path (0/33 correctly quantified before,
        180/180 after) (#9997, #10012)
      - In-process rescoring backend: PercolatorAdapter now defaults to running Percolator in-process
        via the new OpenMS::Percolator class (vendored Percolator 3.08 + TRON solver), so no external
        binary is needed; -percolator_executable became optional. Covers idXML/mzid input with PSM-level
        FDR; OSW input and protein/peptide-level FDR still require -use_subprocess true (#9218)
      - Fix: the -score:fdr and -best_per_spectrum_only post-filters are now also applied on the
        in-process path, which previously produced different output than the subprocess path (#9240)
      - Fix: memory leaks in the in-process backend (Normalizer singleton and Logger file handles)
        (#9257)
    IsobaricWorkflow:
      - Fix: -protein_quantification strictly_unique_peptides no longer aborts with "No information on
        indistinguishable protein groups found.". The setting annotates no protein groups, but
        quantification and all exporters report abundances on them; proteins left without a strictly
        unique peptide are now dropped and the remaining ones reported as singleton groups. Peptide
        uniqueness must be annotated in the input (this tool does not index), which the error message
        now says (#9979)

    ProteomicsLFQ:
      - Fix: loadAndCleanupIDFile_ stripped every meta value except a fixed keep-list before ffi.run(),
        so an input CalcMass (see PercolatorAdapter, above) could never reach FeatureFinderIdentification's
        CalcMass correction even when the upstream search engine provided one. CalcMass is now kept
        (#9998, #10012)
      - New '-feat_dir': a directory of per-run feature checkpoints, making a run resumable and its
        per-run half distributable. One rule covers every case -- for each row of the experimental
        design, reuse its checkpoint if a valid one exists, else detect the run from '-in'/'-ids' and
        write one, else fail. So the same command rerun after an interruption continues where it
        stopped; '-detect_only' on each machine produces the checkpoints; and a final invocation with
        neither '-in' nor '-ids' combines them, reading no mzML, no idXML and no run-level FASTA
        content. A checkpoint records the parameters, OpenMS build, design row and input files behind
        it and is refused when any of those disagree, naming the setting that differs; there is no way
        to combine checkpoints that disagree, and '-force_recompute' detects the affected runs again.
        Inputs are identified by size and modification time rather than by content, so deciding to
        reuse a checkpoint reads nothing. Requires an explicit '-design' (a generated one would label
        every separately detected run as the first) and '-fasta'; not applicable to spectral_counting.
        A split run reproduces a single-shot run: the QPX feature, psm and pg views compare equal
        within 1e-3 relative -- the tolerance already used for independent ProteomicsLFQ processes
        elsewhere, since MSVC shows low-order integration drift between them. '-in'/'-ids' are
        consequently no longer mandatory parameters, but are still required unless '-feat_dir' covers
        every run of the design (#9989)
      - Fix: -protein_quantification strictly_unique_peptides no longer discards every peptide hit.
        The 'protein_references' meta value that marks a peptide as theoretically unique was stripped
        right after peptide indexing, so the filter found nothing to keep; it is now preserved for that
        setting. Proteins left without evidence are dropped and the remaining ones are reported as
        singleton groups, which the quantification and all exporters need (#9979)
      - The chromatographic FWHM the feature linker derives its RT tolerance from is now the median
        over the runs of a fraction. It used to be whichever run the per-file loop happened to end
        on, so the tolerance depended on the order the files were given in. Feature detection is
        unaffected: each run still uses its own FWHM for the FFID peak width. Related: a run that
        cannot measure one (.d/IM_PEAK without seeds) now falls back to the documented 30 s default
        instead of inheriting the previous run's value, and a decoy affix that differs between input
        files is reported rather than silently letting the last file decide the one picked protein
        FDR uses. On the shipped fixtures the fraction values move (8.64/7.78/7.12 s: 7.12 -> 7.78)
        without changing any output, since the tolerance is dominated by the alignment term (#9985)
      - Fix: the quantification-score SVM filter (-feature_with_id_min_score /
        -feature_without_id_min_score) now actually shuffles its training sample before capping it
        at 1000 targets and 1000 decoys; a constructed-but-never-called RandomShuffler left the cap
        applied to the first 1000 of each in RT order, biasing training towards early-eluting
        features. Shuffling uses a fixed seed, so the selection stays reproducible across runs. No
        effect unless one of those two parameters is set above its default of 0.0 (#9981, #9982)
      - Identifications are now reduced to one per (spectrum, peptidoform, charge) when the input
        carries several of them -- the shape produced by concatenating search engine results rather
        than combining them (e.g. PSMFeatureExtractor -multiple_search_engines -concat, or
        PercolatorAdapter at its default -score:fdr 1.0, which retains PSMs it marked as not
        identified). The best-scoring one is kept and the reduction is reported. Keeping all of them
        counted one measurement several times: in the PSM-level FDR, in the mzTab PSM rows, and, with
        -out_qpx, as a duplicate psm_id that the QPX writers refuse. Identifications of one spectrum
        naming different peptidoforms (a chimeric spectrum) are left alone. Combine search engine
        results with ConsensusID (-algorithm best -keep_old_scores) to control this upstream (#9871)
      - The mzTab output (-out) is optional when another output is requested; -out_cxml, -out_qpx,
        or -out_msstats can now be the sole output. Invocations without any output still
        fail during parameter validation (#9866)
      - BREAKING: no normalization is applied, under any combination of output flags. A median scaling
        used to run over each fraction's consensus map unless -out_msstats was requested,
        so the same input written to the same -out_qpx directory held normalized or raw intensities
        depending on whether an unrelated output file was also asked for, and nothing recorded which.
        Normalizing per fraction and then summing across fractions is only ratio-preserving when the
        reference is the same sample in every fraction, which a non-rectangular design does not
        guarantee: on BSA_design_onetable_nonconsec.tsv the scaling moved feature intensities by a
        median factor of 5.3 and a maximum of 12.0. No comparable tool normalizes per fraction against a
        per-fraction reference run (MSstats equalizeMedians and DIA-NN target a global median; MaxQuant
        and FlashLFQ fit the fraction coefficients jointly with the summation). Normalizing is now an
        explicit step: ConsensusMapNormalizer on -out_cxml, the ProteinQuantification:consensus:normalize
        parameter, or the downstream tool's own normalization (#9864, #9880)
      - Added PIP-ECHO match-between-runs via the new PipEchoAlgorithm (-pip_echo true): features are
        grouped across RT-aligned runs with a target/decoy confidence model and FDR-controlled
        identification transfer, with ion-mobility-aware matching (#9647)
      - PIP-ECHO uses a LOCAL adaptive RT window by default: the acceptor RT is predicted per
        (donor run, acceptor run) pair from shared anchor peptides and the window is sized from the local
        RT scatter, rejecting RT-implausible transfers (~19x fewer false transfers into a negative
        control on single-cell PXD016921 at equal genuine recovery). The previous global window is still
        available via -PipEcho:local_rt:enabled false (#9680)
      - Fix: the PIP-ECHO mass_error feature was identically 0.0 because FeatureFinderIdentification
        seeds the feature m/z to the theoretical m/z; it now reads the precomputed masserror_ppm meta
        value. The raw absolute RT difference was also removed from the mbr_score geometric mean, where
        it had inverted monotonicity. On real data PIP-ECHO now performs genuine FDR-controlled MBR
        (1280 transfers at fdr=0.05 vs. 0 before) (#9674)
      - PIP-ECHO now extracts 3 isotopes (up from 2, which gave a degenerate Pearson) and adds an
        SVM-only Pearson correlation between the acceptor's observed and the donor's theoretical isotope
        envelope, so coincidental same-m/z matches score lower (+46% completeness and +36% more transfers
        on PXD016921 relative to the mass-error fix alone) (#9676)
      - Perf: new PipEcho:max_training_points (default 50000, 0=unlimited) caps the transfer-FDR SVM
        training set per fold via a deterministic stratified subsample; prediction and q-values still
        cover all transfers, so FDR estimates stay unbiased (#9669)
      - Added Bruker TimsTOF .d input with Biosaur2-based seed generation (IM_PEAK path with automatic
        FWHM estimation), new Seeding:algorithm parameter (#9030)
      - Added PeptideQuantification:seed_apex_rt_tolerance (default 5.0 s, advanced) to
        FeatureFinderIdentificationAlgorithm; seeds whose detected apex RT deviates further are
        discarded (#9102)
      - Re-enabled tree_guided as a valid -alignment_order value; it had been disabled because the
        MapAlignerTreeGuided transformation was tautological (see MapAligner* below). Default remains
        star (#8752)
      - Fix: mzTab export no longer aborts when search settings contain non-string DataValues (e.g.
        integer missed_cleavages) (#9666)
    IsobaricWorkflow:
      - Identifications are now reduced to one per (spectrum, peptidoform, charge), as for
        ProteomicsLFQ. This tool builds one consensus feature per identification and all
        identifications of one spectrum read the same MS2/MS3 scan, so a repeated identification
        contributed the same reporter intensities twice to every abundance derived from them
        (measured: +37% on a protein abundance from a single duplicated identification) (#9871)
      - The consensusXML (-out), mzTab (-out_mzTab), and QPX (-out_qpx) outputs are optional
        individually; any one can now be the sole output. Invocations without an output still fail
        during parameter validation (#9866)
      - Added TMT 32-plex and 35-plex quantitation; the isotope correction matrix defaults to identity
        until calibrated values are provided (#9003)
      - Optional SPS match counting (-count_sps_matches): counts, per identified PSM, how many
        co-isolated SPS precursors of an SPS-MS3 reporter scan match a theoretical b/y fragment of the
        identified peptide; stored as the 'sps_matched_ions'/'sps_precursor_count' meta values. Off by
        default (#4792, #9706)
      - Fixed mzTab export when processing multiple mzML/idXML pairs (#8519) and handling of MS2 spectra
        without a corresponding MS3 in the SPS-MS3 workflow (#8592)
      - Fix: segfault on any run whose first MS2 has no preceding MS1 (an acquisition starting mid-cycle,
        or an mzML filtered to a spectrum subset). getPrecursorSpectrum()'s "none" return of -1 was used
        as a spectrum index; the precursor purity is now left at the documented -1 "not available" value
        and the feature is quantified without a purity annotation (#9817)
      - Fix: peptide IDs that could not be quantified left default-constructed placeholder features in
        the pre-sized ConsensusMap; they are now pruned explicitly instead of relying on the downstream
        intensity filter, which also dropped real features whose reporter channels were all zero (#9824)
      - Fix: IsobaricChannelExtractor now annotates ConsensusMap::ColumnHeaders with the resolved
        experimental design (fraction_group/fraction/sample), as the label-free path always did, so
        exporters can distinguish two fractions of one sample from two independent runs. New
        ExperimentalDesign::annotateColumnHeaders(), the inverse of fromConsensusMap() (#9836)
    CometAdapter:
      - Merges compatible variable modifications for improved search performance (3x+ speedup) (#8407)
