    PeakPickerIM:
      - Built-in Sage IM centroiding via bruker:ms1_centroid_mz_ppm and bruker:ms1_centroid_im_pct; when
        both are set, centroiding runs on raw gridded TOF data and the IM picking step is skipped.
        IM_SPECTRUM input is rejected with a clear error message (#9022)
      - IM-axis hill centroiding via -algorithm hill (see BrukerTimsFile/PASEFHillCentroider) (#9380)
      - Fix: '-method traces' built its mass traces from a shuffled ion-mobility axis.
        IMDataConverter::reshapeIMFrameToMany emits one spectrum per ion-mobility value in ascending
        order, all carrying the frame's retention time by design, and then called
        MSExperiment::sortSpectra. That sorts by RT with std::sort, which is not stable, so the
        all-equal key was permuted arbitrarily (85 descending steps over 868 spectra were measured
        on the test data). PeakPickerIM reinterprets that axis as RT, and mass-trace detection walks
        spectrum indices, so traces picked up chemically unrelated peaks - one observed trace spanned
        0.823 to 0.919 in 1/K0 against a configured chrom_fwhm of 0.01 - and the output was not
        reproducible across standard-library implementations. Only the per-spectrum m/z sort was
        wanted, so the RT sort is gone; the loop above already emits ascending ion mobility and
        asserts it. Roughly 10% of the picked peaks in the test data turn out to have been spurious
        splits of incoherent traces, so PeakPickerIM_3_output.mzML was regenerated - the previous
        reference encoded one particular libstdc++ shuffle. MSExperiment::sortSpectra itself is
        unchanged; switching it to std::stable_sort would fix every equal-RT map at once and should
        land deliberately rather than as a drive-by (#10051)
    PeakPickerHiRes:
