    - FAIMS-aware adapter for converting pre-loaded MSExperiment/PeakMap data into the existing OpenSWATH SwathMap abstraction (#9932)
  Changes:
    - Fix: AASequence::setModificationByDiffMonoMass (and the four other call sites that write an
      unknown mass-shift modification: three in AASequence, one in Residue, one in PepXMLFile) built
      its ModificationsDB lookup key with a signed bracket but created the modification from an
      unsigned one, e.g. registering "K[306.0253...]" while looking up "K[+306.0253...]". The lookup
      could never hit what creation had just registered, and AASequence::fromString reads an unsigned
      bracket as the residue's absolute mass rather than a delta, so re-parsing written output silently
      produced a different peptidoform and mass (observed on real data: 184/184 written cross-linked
      features round-tripped to the wrong mass). All five sites now use the existing signed
      ResidueModification::getDiffMonoMassString(); the parser, which already accepts both spellings,
      is unchanged. Separately, applying an unknown mass shift to a residue that already carried a
      modification replaced it and interned the result under an id keyed only on the one-letter residue
      code, so e.g. oxidized and plain M given the same nominal shift could be conflated into one
      process-global entry depending on which was processed first. setModificationByDiffMonoMass now
      resolves the unmodified base residue, folds any existing modification's mass into a cumulative
      delta, and interns on that instead, so the two stay distinct and order-independent (#10006,
      #10007, #10010)
    - Fix: QPXFile silently dropped an unsupported modification on Parquet import instead of reporting
      it -- ProForma's BEST_EFFORT mode skips unrecognised tags by design, and an empty catch(...)
      fell through to the unmodified sequence, so e.g. "AEADNLDDK(NuXL:U-H2O)K" (1423.5504 Da) came
      back as "AEADNLDDKK" (1117.5251 Da) with nothing logged even though the row still held the
      peptidoform, the modification name/position and calculated_mz. Conversion issues are now
      reported, the empty catch is replaced with handlers naming the peptidoform and reason, and the
      reconstructed m/z is cross-checked against the row's own calculated_mz. This does not make the
      format carry modification definitions -- reading such a file still loses the modification, just
      no longer silently (#10004, #10012)
    - Fix: OpenNuXL wrote the charge twice into the name of a shifted immonium ion. The generator
      already ends those names with the charge ("iY+U-H3PO4+"), and the annotation step appended
      another one, so the peak was annotated "iY+U-H3PO4++" while PeakAnnotation::charge said 1 -- the
      same defect as #8766, in the written data rather than in the display. The special lysine immonium
      ions had the mirror problem and reached the output with no charge in the name at all
      ("iK(C6H13N2O)"). The charge is now added only when the name does not already carry one. Changes
      the fragment annotations written by OpenNuXL (#8766)
    - Fix: MzPAF could not parse a negative charge. MzPAF::toString() writes the sign it is given
      ("c1^-1"), but parseCharge_() required a digit straight after the caret, so parse() rejected its
      own output with INVALID_CHARGE and no negatively charged ion -- i.e. any nucleic acid fragment --
      could be expressed in mzPAF at all. An optional sign is now read before the number
    - The OpenMS-native Arrow round-trip writers (.consensusparquet, .featureparquet, .idparquet) no
      longer stamp a qpx_version key in the Parquet footer. Those files are not QPX exchange views --
      they use the native schemas and file_type tokens such as consensus_features / features /
      proteins -- and ArrowIOHelpers treats the mere presence of that key as "this table is QPX", so
      claiming it was both misleading and stale (they said 1.0 while the real QPX views say 1.1). The
      QPX exporters are unchanged (#9864)
    - BREAKING: Matrix<T> no longer inherits from Eigen and uses std::vector<T> for storage; Eigen moved
      from PUBLIC to PRIVATE linking, so downstream users no longer need Eigen headers (#8511)
    - BREAKING: the immutable singleton databases return a const pointer from getInstance():
      RibonucleotideDB, MonosaccharideDB, ElementDB, ModificationsDB, CrossLinksDB and (via the
      DigestionEnzymeDB CRTP base) ProteaseDB and RNaseDB; ResidueDB returns const as well, via a
      logical-const cache. Downstream code storing the result in a non-const pointer must switch to
      const T* or auto. Their lookup methods are const-qualified accordingly; the modification database
      interns runtime-encountered modifications into a thread-safe, idempotent pool
    - BREAKING: ElementDB::addElement() was removed -- computing isotope-labeled patterns no longer
      mutates the shared global elements. CoarseIsotopePatternGenerator gained
      setIsotopeOverride(element, distribution) for a local per-element override (default behaviour and
      numeric output unchanged); MetaProSIP and FeatureFinderAlgorithmPicked were migrated to it instead
      of const_cast-ing the globals, which makes them thread-safe and fixes FeatureFinderAlgorithmPicked
      never resetting the globally modified abundances after a run
    - BREAKING: PeptideIdentification::get/setBaseName() removed; the per-spectrum base_name meta value is
      superseded by the run-level primary MS run path (setPrimaryMSRunPath() + IdentifierMSRunMapper).
      PepXMLFile records the source run via setPrimaryMSRunPath(), fixing a latent bug where the path was
      set on a local copy and never reached the stored ProteinIdentification (#9660)
    - BREAKING: transitive Qt and other includes trimmed from the core KERNEL and METADATA headers
      (ConsensusMap.h, FeatureMap.h, ProteinIdentification.h, SpectrumSettings.h, ExperimentalSettings.h);
      downstream code relying on them must include the headers directly (#8946)
    - BREAKING: IDFilter::updateProteinReferences() renamed to removeDanglingProteinReferences() (#8500)
    - BREAKING: Exception::InvalidSize requires a mandatory context message parameter (#8437)
    - BREAKING: FileWatcher moved from OpenMS/SYSTEM/ to OpenMS/VISUAL/ (GUI library) (#8591)
    - BREAKING: PeptideAndProteinQuant channel identifiers changed from Int to UInt (#8516)
    - BREAKING: plain enums migrated to enum class (Sample::SampleState, OpenMS_OS, ...) (#8622)
    - BREAKING: ChromatogramPeak::IntensityType changed from double to float, for consistency with Peak1D
      (#8857)
    - BREAKING: IMFormat enum values renamed for clarity (CONCATENATED -> IM_PEAK, MULTIPLE_SPECTRA ->
      IM_SPECTRUM); IMFormat::MIXED removed and determineIMFormat(MSExperiment) replaced with
      determineIMFormat(MSExperiment, int ms_level) requiring an explicit MS level (#8993, #9011)
    - BREAKING: QuantmsIO renamed to QPXFile; the PSM Parquet export was rewritten using the QPX pattern
      (#8756)
    - BREAKING: OpenSwath::Scoring: the deprecated pointer-based (C-array) overloads of
      NormalizedManhattanDist, RootMeanSquareDeviation, SpectralAngle, normalize_sum and
      calcxcorr_legacy_mquest_ were removed; use the std::vector& overloads (#9252)
    - BREAKING: ChromatogramExtractor::prepare_coordinates(TargetedExperiment) removed; convert via
      OpenSwathDataAccessHelper::convertTargetedExp and use the LightTargetedExperiment overload (the
      pyOpenMS API is unchanged) (#9271)
    - BREAKING: NeighborSeq owns its moved-in peptide vector instead of retaining a dangling const
      reference; this fixes a temporary-argument use-after-free but changes the exported class layout, so
      downstream C++ consumers must rebuild. IDBoostGraph now rejects missing target_decoy annotations
      instead of silently treating them as decoys (#9488, #9613)
    - DELETION: LinearResampler removed; its functionality was refactored into LinearResamplerAlign (#9153)
    - CoarseIsotopePatternGenerator and FineIsotopePatternGenerator silently added 'charge'-many hydrogen
      atoms for a charged input EmpiricalFormula, shifting the pattern without dividing by charge. The
      behaviour is unchanged in 3.x but now deprecated (a one-time warning is logged) and will be removed
      in OpenMS 4.0. The new EmpiricalFormula::addChargeAdduct(count, adduct="H") makes ionization
      explicit as the forward-compatible replacement (#4449, #9714, #9719)
    - OpenSwath::LightCompound gained rt_start and rt_end fields (NaN by default) to carry per-compound RT
      extraction ranges (#9271)
    - BayesianProteinInferenceAlgorithm now requires a single merged ProteinIdentification run (#8480)
    - EMGScoring: default init_mom changed from false to true for consistency (#8593)
    - External Enzymes.xml is now optional -- enzyme definitions are built in by default (#8409)
    - OPENMS_LOG_WARN writes to std::cerr instead of std::cout (#8754); thread-safe logging with
      OPENMS_LOG_* macro optimizations (#8620)
    - TransformationDescription modernized to use std::unique_ptr; Date refactored from QDate inheritance
      to composition (#8710); data provider pattern extracted for ModificationsDB, RibonucleotideDB,
      ProteaseDB and RNaseDB (#8775, #8779)
    - DataValue: enhanced conversion error messages with type and value context (#8572); MetaInfo gained
      iterator support and merge optimizations (#8621); ElementDB map lookup standardized (#8660);
      IDFilter code duplication eliminated (#8662)
    - IDMapper preserves upstream identification data processing metadata when annotating
      featureXML/consensusXML (#8852)
    - OpenSWATH/XIPM Parquet export preserves target/decoy labeling consistently for mixed IPF libraries
      and uses checked Arrow appends for the new chromatogram, mobilogram and peak-map consumers
    - OpenSWATH Parquet readers: the shared Arrow/Parquet plumbing of XICParquetFile and XIMParquetFile
      was deduplicated into one internal helper, so XIC uses the same type-safe extraction path as XIM
      (#9691)
    - OpenSwathOSWWriter gained getRowCount() and estimateMemoryUsage() for memory-aware buffered output
      (#9190)
    - SignalToNoiseEstimatorMedian now interpolates within the histogram bin instead of returning the
      noise estimate of the bin the signal falls into, so S/N varies continuously with intensity rather
      than jumping between a fixed set of values; a hard floor of 1 is preserved. Every downstream
      consumer (peak picking, feature scoring, targeted extraction, OpenSwath/MRM analysis) sees slightly
      smoother, more accurate S/N-derived output; reference test data across ~60 TOPP tests was
      regenerated accordingly (#9781, #9967)
    - Fix: a small signed mass bracket on a residue, e.g. "PEPT[-0.5]IDE" or "PEPT[+0.001]IDE", was
      silently replaced by the zero-mass PSI-MOD term "MOD:00026 L-threonine residue" and the peptide
      lost a water (18.0106 Da). Three causes, all fixed: (a) AASequence::parseModSquareBrackets_
      under-counted the written decimals by one, so every mass bracket was matched ten times more
      loosely than written ("[-0.5]" was matched with a tolerance of 1.0 Da, "[+0.001]" with 0.01 Da);
      it now uses the precision the mass was written with. (b) ModificationsDB's mass searches
      (getBestModificationByDiffMonoMass and the four searchModificationsByDiffMonoMass[Sorted]
      overloads) returned modifications with a difference mass of 0 - PSI-MOD "residue" terms that
      describe a residue rather than a change to it - as the best match for any small mass difference;
      such entries are now only reported when a zero mass difference is what was searched for.
      (c) Residue::setModification adopted the absolute mass and formula of a modification that
      describes no difference at all; since PSI-MOD stores the residue as it occurs inside a peptide
      chain, i.e. without the water of the free amino acid that Residue stores, this stripped a water
      whenever such a term was applied - including via "(MOD:00026)" written into idXML. Note that
      (a) also tightens matching of coarsely written masses: "M[147.01]" (25 mDa off oxidized
      methionine, more than the 0.01 Da implied by two decimals) and other masses written with fewer
      decimals than their deviation from the database entry are now kept as exact unknown mass shifts
      instead of being snapped to a named modification. (b) also affects the file readers that resolve
      modifications by mass: a near-zero mass shift, e.g. the C-terminal "massdiff" of 0.0003 that
      X!Tandem writes into pepXML, no longer picks up an unrelated zero-mass PSI-MOD term - such
      peptides used to be annotated with a spurious "(MOD:00266)" (#10029)
    - Fix: MassTrace::estimateFWHM reported a wrong FWHM. Interpolating the right flank to the
      half-maximum crossing paired each RT with the *other* bracketing point's intensity, so the
      crossing came out mirrored about the midpoint of its bracket: a symmetric trace with
      intensities {10, 60, 100, 60, 10} at RT 0..4 was reported as FWHM 3.0 (right crossing at 3.8)
      instead of 2.4 (right crossing at 3.2). Only the right flank was affected; the left flank
      paired its coordinates correctly, and a trace that never drops below half maximum on the right
      was already correct because it does not interpolate. The private helper
      linearInterpolationAtY_ no longer assumes that x ascends with y - its postcondition bracketed
      the result as xA <= xC <= xB, which is wrong for a descending x pair, and now checks
      min(xA, xB) <= xC <= max(xA, xB). The reflection keeps the crossing inside its bracketing
      pair, so the FWHM moved by up to one sampling interval in either direction (and was exact
      only when the crossing happened to fall on the bracket's midpoint). estimateFWHM also gained
      an explicit precondition that the trace is sorted along its x axis: the flank search walks
      peak indices outward from the apex, and a shuffled axis (#10051) used to be caught only
      indirectly, when it happened to hand the interpolation a descending bracketing pair.
      Corrected FWHMs change results downstream: FeatureFindingMetabo's isotope co-elution score
      and the width (the "FWHM" user parameter) it writes onto each feature, ElutionPeakDetection's
      fixed and auto (quantile-based) peak-width filters, the width MassTraceExtractor writes, and
      - through DDAWorkflowCommons::estimateMedianChromatographicFWHM, which medians the FWHM of
      the first 1000 mass traces of a run - ProteomicsLFQ's RT linking tolerance,
      FeatureFinderIdentification peak widths and FeatureFinderMultiplex seed detection
      (algorithm:rt_typical = 3x and algorithm:rt_min = 0.5x that median). On the BSA test data
      that median moves 7.779 -> 7.330 s and 8.684 -> 8.465 s, and the quantified intensities move
      with it: across the updated MSstats CSVs the median relative change is ~1e-4, but the
      distribution has a long tail - 20 of 188 changes exceed 1% and the largest is 8.55% - and
      the seed-based runs gain consensus features (198 -> 204 for the FeatureFinderMetabo-seeded
      ProteomicsLFQ test). Reference outputs of the FeatureFinderMetabo, MassTraceExtractor and
      ProteomicsLFQ tests were updated accordingly (#10052)
  Performance:
    - ILPDCWrapper ~3.5x faster using std::unordered_map (#8618); unordered_map lookups in OpenSwath
      (#8651), PrecursorPurity, PeptideProteinResolution and PeptideIndexing (#8763, #8806)
    - Vectorized dotprodScoring/manhattanScoring in StatsHelpers (#8848) and cross-correlation in
      OpenSwath Scoring (#8849) using Eigen; OpenSwath scoring speedups: capped XCorr maxdelay,
      vectorized DIAScoring, hoisted loop-invariants, SwathMap passed by const& (#8785, #8787)
    - AccurateMassSearchEngine: with use_feature_adducts set, only the observed adduct is evaluated
      instead of the full list (#8635, fixes #5867)
    - MzIdentML parser: CV lookups cached; mzIdentML 1.3 schema support added (#8764)
    - Parquet identification I/O: the additional_scores and psm_metavalues columns are built in a single
      pass per PeptideHit (~20-25% faster on metavalue-heavy PSMs), and reading drops a per-row
      value_slice() allocation; QPXFile resolves per-PSM metavalues via metaBegin()/metaEnd() with a
      local index cache, so the MetaInfoRegistry lock is taken at most once per unique key per export
      instead of twice per metavalue per PSM. Output is round-trip identical (#9689, #9692)
    - Removed avoidable heavy copies in OpenMP regions and hot loops across OPXLHelper::digestDatabase,
      MultiplexFilteringProfile, MzMLSqliteHandler, SwathFileConsumer, SimpleSearchEngineAlgorithm,
      ProSEAlgorithm and NuXLAnnotateAndLocate (#9686)
    - ProSEAlgorithm: TheoreticalSpectrumGenerator and SpectrumAlignment used for post-scoring peak
      annotation are now built once per run instead of per PSM, a redundant open-search
      modification-analysis pass whose only effect was logging was dropped, each spectrum's candidate
      list is pruned to max(report:top_hits, 2) as soon as scoring moves past it instead of staying at
      scoring:max_candidates_per_spectrum, and the calibration pass reuses one spectrum buffer across
      candidates. Output-identical; closed search ~26% and open search ~30% faster (#9963)
    - PrecursorCorrection::correctToNearestFeature() replaced the Cartesian product over precursors and
      features (8e8 overlap checks, each rebuilding a non-memoized bounding box) with one precomputed
      bounding box per feature and a sweep line over increasing precursor m/z (#4787, #9801)
    - Iterator patterns, container checks and memory allocation optimized in hot paths (#8656)

Fixes:
  - Fix: Biosaur2Algorithm::run() left the stored MS data empty on FAIMS input -- the per-CV split moves
    the spectra out of ms_data_ and nothing put them back, so getMSData() handed the caller a cleared
    experiment. ProteomicsLFQ with -Seeding:algorithm biosaur2 passed exactly that on to
    FeatureFinderIdentification, which crashed on the zero-spectrum experiment (its chromatogram
    extraction indexes the PeakMap unconditionally). The spectra and chromatograms are now reassembled
    in acquisition order (profile-mode centroiding likewise no longer replaces the whole experiment,
    which dropped the chromatograms and experimental settings before the split saw them), so both
    Biosaur2 paths leave the same processed experiment behind, and
    FeatureFinderIdentificationAlgorithm::run() rejects MS data without spectra instead of reading out
    of bounds (#9980, #9996)
  - Fix: ExperimentalDesign's Sample* mappings disagreed about their key, so any design whose sample names
    are not "0", "1", ... crashed with a bare std::out_of_range. getSampleToPrefractionationMapping() was
    keyed by sample name while the condition and path-label mappings used the stringified zero-based row
    index; all are now keyed by sample name. Only designs OpenMS infers itself (where name and index
    coincide) worked before (#9874)
  - Fix: Parquet writers left a partial file behind when a write failed -- arrow::io::FileOutputStream::Open
    truncates the file up front, so a later failure leaves a fragment with no footer that reads as corrupt.
    ProteinIdentificationArrowIO, FeatureMapArrowIO, ConsensusMapArrowIO, ParquetFile::writeTable and the
    QPX streaming writer now remove the incomplete output (#9862)
  - Fix: IDFilter::updateProteinGroups rebuilt each protein group from scratch, keeping only accessions and
    probability and discarding the data arrays, so any tool filtering after quantification (Epifany,
    ProteinInference, FalseDiscoveryRate, PercolatorAdapter, ProteomicsLFQ) threw the protein quantities
    away. ProteinIdentification::ProteinGroup::operator== also ignored the data arrays, so a round-trip
    test passed even when all of them had been dropped
  - Fix: MSChromatogram and Mobilogram silently mis-associated per-peak metadata when sorted, because their
    sort routines did not permute all binary data arrays alongside the peaks; sortByIntensity() guarded on
    an inverted condition, taking the metadata-ignoring fast path precisely when string and integer arrays
    were present. Reachable on the mzML load path (#9795)
  - Fix: 14 boundary/serialization/state-consistency defects from an implementation-consistency audit,
    including an empty-mass-range dereference in QTClusterFinder/FeatureGroupingAlgorithmKD, unsigned
    loop-bound underflows in TheoreticalSpectrumGenerator::addInternalFragmentPeaks_ and MzTab's
    nextPSMRow (both effectively infinite loops), and an unbounded quote-skipping loop in
    OMSSACSVFile::load (#9767, #9790)
  - Fix: DistanceMatrix::setValue's fast-path cache-invalidation guard used && instead of ||, so the cached
    minimum went stale; DistanceMatrix::operator== performed an out-of-bounds heap read for differently
    sized matrices; ClusteringGrid::getClusters no longer dereferences map::end() for unpopulated cells
    (#9488, #9497, #9610)
  - Fix: ROCCurve::cutoffNeg() iterated positive samples while dividing by the negative sample count; it
    now iterates negative samples to match its denominator (#9488, #9617)
  - Fix: FragmentMassError::compute()'s PeptideIdentificationList overload computed the ppm-error variance
    against a moving average, disagreeing with the FeatureMap overload for the same data; both now produce
    identical average_ppm/variance_ppm (#9488, #9618)
  - Fix: LogStreamBuf thread-safety -- the static buffer, the sink list and Colorizer were unprotected
    against concurrent access from OpenMP regions, which could corrupt the heap (e.g. STATUS_HEAP_CORRUPTION
    in the imzML reader on Windows) (#9583, fixes #9515)
  - Fix: ProSE and SimpleSearchEngine no longer crash (SIGABRT from an exception escaping an OpenMP region)
    when the fragment tolerance exceeds the range supported by deisotoping (> 100 ppm or > 0.1 Da); the
    step is now guarded by Deisotoper::isToleranceSupported() (#9620, fixes #9619)
  - Fix: TransitionPQPFile and MzMLSqliteHandler concatenated untrusted strings (transition library
    compound/peptide/protein/gene names, SMILES, formulas, adducts, annotations; loaded file paths) directly
    into INSERT statements executed via sqlite3_exec, so a single quote broke the statement and a crafted
    value could inject SQL into the written .pqp/.sqMass output. Both now prepare the statements once and
    bind the values per row (#9730, #9734)
  - Fix: TargetedExperiment::getProteinByRef/getPeptideByRef/getCompoundByRef used unordered_map::operator[]
    for unknown refs, inserting and then dereferencing a null pointer; they now throw
    Exception::ElementNotFound. The reference-map caches also clear stale pointers on rebuild and are
    invalidated by setCompounds()/addCompound(), and ReactionMonitoringTransition::getPrediction() no longer
    checks the wrong predicate before dereferencing a null Prediction (#9488, #9492)
  - Fix: EmgFitter1D::fit1d() (the elution-model-fit score in OpenSWATH's MRMFeatureFinderScoring and in
    FeatureFinderIdentification) could read out of bounds, throw UnableToFit into callers or return NaN
    parameters on degenerate input; it now guards degenerate input and non-finite optimizer output and falls
    back to a stable in-range model with the existing -1.0 quality sentinel (#6239, #9682)
  - Fix: SimpleSVM::predict() misaligned LIBSVM feature indices when constant predictors were dropped during
    training, causing silently wrong predictions or out-of-bounds crashes; setup() crashed when the
    alphabetically first predictor was constant, and an all-constant input now throws MissingInformation
    (#9661, #9670)
  - Fix: SpectraSTSimilarityScore::dot_bias() could still return NaN for orthogonal or all-zero binned
    spectra; it now returns 0.0 when the recomputed denominator is non-positive (#9495, #9594)
  - Fix: Math::quantile1st/quantile3rd threw InvalidRange for input ranges of size 1 or 2 and now return
    min/max for n < 3, consistent with the n=3/4 behaviour; SummaryStatistics::variance returns 0.0 for n==1
    instead of NaN. The n >= 3 path is byte-identical (#9659, #9664)
  - Fix: TheoreticalSpectrumGenerator: corrected m/z for b/a neutral-loss fragment ions; the neutral-loss
    loop omitted the first residue mass (#9078, #9084)
  - Fix: LinearResampler: right_index was clamped to the number of raw points instead of the number of
    resampled points, causing extreme intensity spikes when resampled points far outnumber raw points; bug
    introduced in 2008 and masked by LinearResamplerAlign (#9127)
  - Fix: ParamXMLHandler parsed the optional 'required' attribute of <ITEM> into the variable holding the
    parsed 'advanced' value and tested the same variable, so any advanced ITEM omitting 'required' was
    silently tagged required. Reached by hand-written and third-party INI files and by files written against
    the Param 1.0-1.2 schemas (#9763)
  - Fix: XQuestResultXMLHandler read the 'fdr_type' attribute into the variable holding the previously parsed
    'xprophet_f' value and guarded the assignment on a string that was never written, so XFDR:fdr_type could
    never be restored from a .xquest.xml file (#9763, fixes #9766)
  - Fix: reading mzIdentML no longer crashes with IndexOverflow when a <Modification> omits the optional
    'location' attribute (e.g. Philosopher output for N-terminal acetylation); the position is inferred from
    the modification's terminal specificity, but only when no concrete 'residues' amino acid is named (#5443)
  - Fix: PepXMLFile no longer stores six Comet scores twice under redundant, differently-cased metavalue keys;
    only the canonical keys remain (COMET:deltaCn is unaffected, as PercolatorFeatureSetHelper consumes it)
    (#9089)
  - Fix: ProtXMLFile filters out ProteinProphet "unneeded" (subset/subsumable, probability=0) proteins during
    parsing instead of creating a separate indistinguishable group for each. Those spurious singleton groups
    made ProteinQuantifier discard shared peptides, quantifying most proteins in only one TMT plex (#8960,
    fixes #6038)
  - Fix: imzML duplicate pixel coordinates no longer abort the load or store; they are reported as a warning
    and only the first spectrum per pixel enters the imaging geometry, while every spectrum stays reachable
    by index (#9903)
  - Fix: ChromatogramExtractor::prepare_coordinates (TargetedExperiment overload) honours the ms1_isotopes
    parameter again; a `&& false` guard introduced in 2018 silently disabled the isotope-coordinate block
    (#7284, #9270)
  - Fix: SwathMapMassCorrection: removed a null-pointer dereference and memory leak in correctMZ/correctIM
    when fetchSpectrumSwath returns an empty spectrum (#9386)
  - Fix: OpenSwathParquetExporter finalized every Arrow builder with Finish().ValueOrDie(), which aborts the
    process on an Arrow error; it now uses the shared appendOrThrow()/finishArray() helpers and throws
    Exception::InvalidValue naming the offending column (#9739, #9778)
  - Fix: disable auto_irt and ignore tr_irt_priority_sampling when linear_irt_file is provided in
    OpenSwathWorkflow (#8830)
  - Fix: massdev_score denominator counts only observed transitions instead of all (#8831)
  - Fix: OpenSwathWorkflow run_id mismatches between Parquet/mzML XIC files and OSW/sqMass output (#8833) and
    between the FEATURE and RUN tables (#8869); batch counting and loop bounds for empty compound sets (#8790)
  - Fix: empty GENE table caused PQP file reading to return zero transitions (#8688)
  - Fix: MSExperiment::reset() now clears chromatograms as well as spectra; they were previously left intact,
    causing duplicate chromatogram entries on reset-and-reload (#9211)
  - Fix: ProteinIdentification::get*DataArrayByName threw undefined behaviour for a missing name; it now
    throws ElementNotFound
  - Fix: ArrowIOHelpers::generateUuidV4() reseeded a fresh local std::mt19937 on every call, which produced
    duplicate UUIDs when the runner's random_device entropy was weak; it now delegates to the new
    UniqueIdGenerator::getUUID() drawing from the shared, once-seeded process RNG (#9703)
  - Fix: UniqueIdGenerator seed collision in parallel pipelines, by mixing the process ID into the seed
    (#8678)
  - Fix: UpdateCheck no longer crashes TOPP tools when $HOME is not writable (e.g. $HOME=/ in containers);
    the check is skipped gracefully (#9074, #9075)
  - Fix: EICExtractor's 'rt_collect' and MetaProSIP's 'heatmap_bins' were registered without a lower bound
    and stored in unsigned types, so a negative or zero value wrapped to a huge count and caused
